- How would you handle a tenant with drift but small user base?
- How would you scale drift computation to thousands of tenants?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to encode business rules (privacy budgets), implement deterministic fallbacks, and design practical APIs.

## Key Concepts

- Daily privacy budget accounting per user
- Deterministic fallback scoring when budget is exhausted
- Idempotent endpoints for reproducibility
- Observability of budget usage

## Code Example

```javascript
function scoreOrFallback(userId, budgetRemaining, modelScore) {
  if (budgetRemaining > 0) {
    return { useModel: true, score: modelScore };
  }
  // deterministic heuristic using user hash as seed
  const seed = hash(userId);
  return { useModel: false, score: deterministicRandom(seed) };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Adds a practical multi-tenant privacy control layer to a beginner API, highlighting data isolation, per-tenant budgets, and simple governance in a real system.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy isolation
- Per-tenant privacy budgets (epsilon, delta)
- Header validation (X-tenant-id)
- Tenant-scoped configuration
- Observability and audits

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: tenant-aware config lookup (Express)
function tenantMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
  if (!tenant) return res.status(400).send('Missing X-tenant-id');
  req.tenant = loadTenantConfig(tenant); // returns {epsilon, delta, ...}
  if (!req.tenant) return res.status(403).send('Unknown tenant');
  next();
}
```

